/* book Worked example: Q: The sequence x[n] = (0.5)ⁿu[n] has X(z) = z/(z − 0.5) with ROC |z| > 0.5. Use the multiplication-by-n property to find the Z-transform of y[n] = n(0.5)ⁿu[n] and state its ROC. Evaluate X(z) and Y(z) at z = 2 as a numerical check.
   1. Write X(z) in closed form: X(z) = z/(z − 0.5), from the geometric sum 1/(1 − 0.5z⁻¹). The pole sits at z = 0.5, so the ROC is |z| > 0.5.
   2. Pick the rule. y[n] = n·x[n], so use n·x[n] ↔ −z·dX/dz. This rule leaves the ROC unchanged.
   3. Differentiate. X(z) = z/(z − 0.5), so dX/dz = [(z − 0.5)·1 − z·1]/(z − 0.5)² = −0.5/(z − 0.5)².
   4. Multiply by −z, giving 0.5z/(z − 0.5)².
   5. State the ROC. The double pole is at z = 0.5, so Y(z) converges for |z| > 0.5.
   6. Check at z = 2. X(2) = 2/1.5 = 1.3333 and Y(2) = 1/(1.5)² = 0.4444.
   7. Sanity check with the initial value theorem. Y(z) → 0 as z → ∞, and y[0] = 0·(0.5)⁰ = 0.
   Answer: y[n] = n(0.5)ⁿu[n] ↔ Y(z) = 0.5z/(z − 0.5)², ROC |z| > 0.5; at z = 2, X(2) = 1.3333 and Y(2) = 0.4444. */
