/* book: lead: The inverse Z-transform recovers the sequence x[n] from its z-domain expression X(z). The algebra alone is not enough: the region of convergence (ROC) fixes whether each pole produces a right-sided or a left-sided term. The formal inverse is a contour integral, but three practical methods do the work: power series, partial fractions and residues. Engineers use it to turn a transfer function or a d */
/* book Figure 6.6.1: For X(z) = 1/((1 − 0.5z⁻¹)(1 − 2z⁻¹)) with ROC 0.5 < |z| < 2, the contour C sits in the ring: the pole at 0.5 is inside C and gives −(1/3)(0.5)ⁿu[n]; the pole at 2 is outside and gives −(4/3)2ⁿu[−n−1]. */
