/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The formal inverse is a contour integral.
   - x[n] = (1/(2πj)) ∮C X(z) zn−1 dz
   - C is a closed counterclockwise contour inside the ROC; j = √(−1); n is the sample index; X(z) is the z-transform.
   - Direct integration is rare.
   - Partial fractions turn X(z) into first-order terms whose inverse pairs are known.
   - The same term 1/(1 − 0.5z⁻¹) gives 0.5ⁿu[n] when |z| > 0.5, and −0.5ⁿu[−n−1] when |z| < 0.5.
   - The residue method sums residues of X(z) zn−1 at the poles inside C.
   - x[n] = Σ Res[X(z) zn−1 at poles inside C]
   - the sum runs over every pole of X(z) zn−1 that lies inside the contour C.
   - For a pole of order m at z = p, use the repeated-pole formula: Res = (1/(m−1)!) limz→p dm−1/dzm−1 [(z − p)m X(z) zn−1].
*/
