/* book Worked example: Q: Find x[n] for X(z) = 1/((1 − 0.5z⁻¹)(1 − 2z⁻¹)) with ROC 0.5 < |z| < 2.
   1. Write X(z) = z²/[(z − 0.5)(z − 2)]. The poles are at z = 0.5 and z = 2.
   2. Form X(z)/z = z/[(z − 0.5)(z − 2)] = A/(z − 0.5) + B/(z − 2).
   3. A = (z − 0.5) X(z)/z at z = 0.5 = 0.5/(0.5 − 2) = −1/3.
   4. B = (z − 2) X(z)/z at z = 2 = 2/(2 − 0.5) = 4/3.
   5. Then X(z) = (−1/3)/(1 − 0.5z⁻¹) + (4/3)/(1 − 2z⁻¹).
   6. The ROC has 0.5 < |z|, so the pole at 0.5 is right-sided: term is −(1/3)(0.5)ⁿu[n].
   7. The ROC has |z| < 2, so the pole at 2 is left-sided: the left-sided pair gives (4/3)·(−2ⁿu[−n−1]) = −(4/3)·2ⁿu[−n−1].
   8. Add the two terms. Check two values: x[0] = −1/3 = −0.333 and x[−1] = −(4/3)(1/2) = −0.667; both sides shrink away from n = 0, so the sequence is absolutely summable, as an ROC containing |z| = 1 requires.
   Answer:  */
