/* book Worked example 2: Q: Find the first four samples of x[n] for X(z) = (1 + 2z−1)/(1 − 0.5z−1), ROC |z| > 0.5, by long division. Then find the closed form by partial fractions and check that the two agree.
   1. ROC |z| > 0.5 means right-sided, so divide to get a series in z−1.
   2. First term: 1. Remainder (1 + 2z−1) − 1·(1 − 0.5z−1) = 2.5z−1.
   3. Next term: 2.5z−1. Remainder 2.5z−1 − 2.5z−1(1 − 0.5z−1) = 1.25z−2.
   4. Next terms: 1.25z−2, then 0.625z−3; each remainder is half the last one. So x[0] = 1, x[1] = 2.5, x[2] = 1.25, x[3] = 0.625.
   5. Partial fractions: write X(z) = A + B/(1 − 0.5z−1). Then A(1 − 0.5z−1) + B = 1 + 2z−1, so −0.5A = 2 gives A = −4, and A + B = 1 gives B = 5.
   6. Inverse: x[n] = −4δ[n] + 5(0.5)nu[n].
   7. Check: x[0] = −4 + 5 = 1, x[1] = 5 × 0.5 = 2.5, x[2] = 5 × 0.25 = 1.25, x[3] = 5 × 0.125 = 0.625. Both methods agree.
   Answer: x[n] = −4δ[n] + 5(0.5)nu[n]: 1, 2.5, 1.25, 0.625, … for n = 0, 1, 2, 3. */
