/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A discrete-time LTI system obeys Σk=0N ak y[n-k] = Σk=0M bk x[n-k].
   - H(z) = Y(z)/X(z) = (Σk=0M bk z−k) / (Σk=0N ak z−k)
   - Y(z) and X(z) are z-transforms of output and input; ak, bk are real coefficients; M and N are non-negative integers; H(z) is the transfer function.
   - The denominator roots are poles; numerator roots are zeros.
   - Causality means h[n] = 0 for n < 0.
   - For a causal system the ROC is |z|>r.
   - The same H(z) with |z|>0.5 gives h[n]=(0.5)nu[n], causal and stable.
   - If the ROC contains |z|=1, put z=ejω.
   - yss[n] = A |H(ejω₀)| cos(ω₀ n + φ + ∠H(ejω₀))
   - A is input amplitude; ω₀ is input frequency in rad/sample; φ is input phase in radians; yss is the steady-state output; the system must be stable.
*/
