/* book exam traps: Reading stability from pole positions alone: stability means the ROC contains |z|=1; for a causal system every pole must be strictly inside the unit circle. | Marking a causal system stable when a pole sits exactly on |z|=1. The ROC then starts at that circle and does not contain it, so BIBO stability fails. | Using H(ejω) when the ROC excludes the unit circle. The algebra still evaluates, but it is not a steady-state frequency response. | Dropping the y[−1] term in one-sided transforms. y[n−1] becomes z⁻¹Y⁺(z)+y[−1], not just z⁻¹Y⁺(z). | Assuming 1/H(z) is stable because H(z) is. Zeros of H outside the unit circle become unstable poles of the causal inverse. */
